<BaseLayout
  title="Arriving in Italy from the United States"
  description="Why 90 days visa-free is not a relocation route, which consulate has your state, the apostille chain for US documents, when you get a codice fiscale, the one-year driving clock, and shipping your household."
  alternates={{ en: '/from/united-states/arriving', it: '/it/from/united-states/arriving' }}
  ogImage="/og/from-us-arriving.png"
  citations={sources}
>
  <nav class="mb-6 text-sm text-muted" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/" class="hover:text-ink">Home</a>
    <span class="px-1">/</span>
    <a href="/from/united-states" class="hover:text-ink">From the United States</a>
    <span class="px-1">/</span>
    <span class="text-ink">Arriving</span>
  </nav>

  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Arriving in Italy from the United States</h1>
    <p>
      The single most expensive American misconception about moving to Italy is
      that the 90-day visa waiver is a way in. It is not, and the reason is
      structural rather than bureaucratic. Facts verified
      <time datetime="2026-07-30">30 July 2026</time>; orientation, not legal or
      immigration advice.
    </p>

    <h2>Ninety days visa-free is not a relocation route</h2>
    <p>
      A US passport gets you into the Schengen area for up to 90 days in any 180
      without a visa. For anything longer you need a <strong>type D national
      long-stay visa</strong> (91–365 days), and Italian law places its issuance
      with the consular mission <strong>in your state of origin or stable
      residence</strong> — that is, in the United States, before you travel.
    </p>
    <p>
      You cannot convert a tourist entry into residence once you are here, and
      this is not discretion but mechanics: the <em>permesso di soggiorno</em> is
      issued "<strong>for the activities provided for by the entry visa</strong>",
      and residence is permitted only to those who entered regularly holding such
      a permit. A visa-free tourist arrival carries no visa, so there is nothing
      for the Questura to issue a permit against. The only in-country flexibility
      extends a <em>short</em> stay; it is not a path to residence. Overstaying is
      a ground for administrative expulsion.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once you do arrive on a type D visa, the clock is short: apply for the
      permesso di soggiorno at the Questura <strong>within eight working days</strong>.
      Start from our <a href="/non-eu">non-EU orientation</a> for the visa
      categories themselves.
    </p>

    <h2>ETIAS and EES: what is actually true today</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>ETIAS is not in operation.</strong> As of this page's verification
      date both the official EU ETIAS site and the European Commission state that
      the system is not running and <em>no applications are being collected</em>.
      Anyone selling you an ETIAS today is running a scam. When it does launch it
      will cost <strong>€20</strong> — and it will be a <em>short-stay</em>
      authorisation from which holders of a long-stay visa or residence permit are
      <strong>exempt</strong>. It was never the relocation document.
    </p>
    <p>
      What <em>has</em> changed is the <strong>Entry/Exit System</strong>: since
      <strong>10 April 2026</strong> it has replaced passport stamping,
      registering biometrics and every entry and exit. The practical consequence
      is that the 90/180 count is now automatic rather than a border officer's
      arithmetic, so an overstay that once passed unnoticed is now detected by
      default.
    </p>

    <h2>Which consulate has you</h2>
    <p>
      Jurisdiction is by <strong>US state — and sometimes by county</strong>. New
      York covers New York, Connecticut, Bermuda and a named list of New Jersey
      counties; Chicago covers eleven midwestern and mountain states. You must
      apply to the one that has your address, prove it with a driver's licence or
      state ID, appear <strong>in person by appointment</strong> for biometrics,
      and sign the form in front of the visa officer. Appointments run through the
      foreign ministry's <strong>Prenot@mi</strong> portal, and New York requires
      you to confirm the booking 3 to 10 days beforehand.
    </p>
    <p>
      We verified New York and Chicago directly; the other consulates publish
      their own jurisdictions, so check yours rather than assuming.
    </p>

    <h2>Your US documents need a three-step chain</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-sm">
      <tbody>
        {
          documents.map(([step, detail]) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
              <td class="py-2.5 pr-4 font-medium whitespace-nowrap">{step}</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 text-muted">{detail}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-6">
    <p>
      A US <strong>notarisation is not a substitute</strong> at any step: a notary
      attests a signature, not a translation's accuracy, and has no standing
      before Italian authorities. The apostille replaces consular
      <em>legalisation</em>; it does not replace the <em>translation</em>. Both
      the US and Italy have been party to the Hague Apostille Convention for
      decades, which is why the apostille route exists at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do this <strong>before</strong> the visa appointment, not after: family
      applications already require an apostilled, translated marriage certificate
      and children's birth certificates at the visa stage. The same documents
      reappear later for
      <a href="/eu-citizens/residency/iscrizione-anagrafica">anagrafe registration</a>,
      <a href="/citizenship">citizenship</a> applications and
      <a href="/schools">school enrolment</a>, so order several certified copies
      while you are in the country that issues them.
    </p>

    <h2>Codice fiscale: plan for on arrival, not before</h2>
    <p>
      The Revenue Agency does say non-residents may apply through Italian consular
      authorities abroad, and that is true — but the Chicago consulate limits it to
      cases where the code is needed for an online procedure or where you are
      objectively unable to appoint a representative in Italy, explicitly
      excluding study, property purchase, "stay in Italy" and litigation. The
      mainstream routes are a <strong>delegated representative</strong> filing at
      an Agenzia delle Entrate office, or — for most people actually relocating —
      assignment <strong>in Italy</strong> by the Sportello Unico or the Questura
      when your permit is issued. See the
      <a href="/eu-citizens/residency/codice-fiscale">codice fiscale guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Your licence has a one-year shelf life</h2>
    <p>
      There is <strong>no reciprocity agreement</strong> between the US and Italy:
      on the transport ministry's list the United States appears only among states
      whose licences convert for <strong>diplomatic staff and their families</strong>.
      Everyone else must pass the Italian theory and practical exams plus a medical.
      For the first <strong>year from acquiring residence</strong> you may drive on
      your valid US licence provided you also carry an
      <strong>International Driving Permit</strong> (issued in the US by AAA) or an
      official Italian translation of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The detail that costs people a year: the US Embassy dates that clock from
      your <strong>registration with the anagrafe</strong>. The year you spend
      settling in is the year you should already be in a driving school. Full
      detail on <a href="/driving">driving on a foreign licence</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Shipping your household</h2>
    <p>
      EU law admits personal property free of import duty and VAT when you
      transfer your normal residence from outside the EU — and "personal property"
      expressly includes private motor vehicles. The relief is conditional:
      you must have <strong>owned and used</strong> the goods for at least
      <strong>6 months</strong> before leaving, have lived outside the EU for at
      least <strong>12 continuous months</strong>, clear the goods within
      <strong>12 months</strong> of establishing residence, and not sell, lend or
      pledge them for <strong>12 months</strong> afterwards without telling
      customs. Alcohol, tobacco, commercial vehicles and trade equipment get no
      relief.
    </p>
    <p>
      One distinction worth keeping separate: duty and VAT relief on
      <em>importing</em> a car is not permission to <em>register</em> it.
      Homologation and registration are a different regime, and we have not
      verified that a US-specification vehicle can be plated in Italy — treat
      those as two questions, and get the second answered before shipping a car.
    </p>

    <h2>Staying American from Italy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Voting</strong>: file a Federal Post Card Application to get absentee ballots — and refile <strong>every January</strong> or whenever you move. Electronic delivery gets you a blank ballot 45 days before general elections.</li>
      <li><strong>Selective Service</strong>: men aged 18 through 25 must register, including dual citizens and citizens living abroad.</li>
      <li><strong>STEP</strong>: enrol with the embassy for security alerts.</li>
      <li><strong>Federal Benefits Unit</strong> in Rome handles Social Security, VA, Medicare and Railroad Retirement matters. Money questions are on our <a href="/from/united-states/taxes">US taxes page</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Babies born in Italy</strong> to a US-citizen parent may acquire citizenship at birth — apply for a Consular Report of Birth Abroad.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <section class="mt-10 rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface/60 p-6">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-xl">Sources</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">
      Primary, official sources this page is based on — each fetched and checked on 30 July 2026.
    </p>
